{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"# S4 — Bound `/var/tmp` growth and alarm on tmp saturation\n\n## Why\n`/var/tmp` was 3.6 GiB of the 28G root working set at the 2026-09-02 outage. Age-only cleanup is\nnot enough if a generator can fill the disk before files age out, and aggregate root alarms do\nnot identify tmp saturation.\n\n## The design ambiguity, resolved\n\n`/var/tmp` is a directory on the **root XFS** filesystem (`/dev/nvme0n1p1`). It has **no\nwriter-enforced cap** — unlike journald's `SystemMaxUse` (S3) or dockerd's `builder.gc` (S2) —\nand `systemd-tmpfiles` has **no size verb for a directory on an ordinary filesystem** (its\n`q`/`Q` lines carry btrfs qgroup limits and do nothing on XFS). So the byte budget is held by\ntwo mechanisms of unequal strength, and **which one is live is published as a metric** rather\nthan asserted by a runbook:\n\n1. **XFS project quota** (`bhard`) is the real ceiling — kernel `EDQUOT`, no fill-rate\n   assumption. It is **operator-enabled**: XFS reads quota mount options at mount time and\n   refuses to enable accounting on a remount, so on the root filesystem it needs\n   `rootflags=pquota` in `GRUB_CMDLINE_LINUX` plus a **reboot** (a scheduled Gerrit outage). The\n   installer applies the limit only when the kernel is already accounting, and otherwise prints\n   the exact steps.\n2. **Age cleanup** via a `tmpfiles.d` drop-in — bounds age, never bytes.\n3. **A bounded oldest-first reaper timer** — what actually holds the budget today. It is a\n   **mitigation with a fill-rate assumption**, not a ceiling: between two 5-minute runs\n   `/var/tmp` is bounded only by the volume, so the enforced bound is\n   `cap + fill_rate x interval`. At 4 GiB and 300 s a sustained net fill above ~14.6 MB/s\n   defeats it, and this gp3 volume does 125 MB/s. It also never evicts inside a grace window,\n   so a burst of fresh files is unreclaimable by design.\n\nRejected: a loop-mounted fixed-size image at `/var/tmp` (permanently consumes its size, hides\nfiles open across the mount, and a sparse image on a full root shuts the image filesystem down);\nand binding `/var/tmp` onto the S1 gate-scratch volume (moves bytes rather than bounding them,\nand while that volume is unmounted — bug `dcc3-75ee-26ce-4840` — would fail open onto root).\n\n## Defaults\nByte budget **4 GiB** (`VAR_TMP_MAX_BYTES`), age **7 d** (`VAR_TMP_MAX_AGE_DAYS`, tightening\nAL2023's shipped 30d), reaper grace window **900 s** (`VAR_TMP_MIN_AGE_SECONDS`). 4 GiB sits\neither side of the 3.6 GiB observed: above it so steady state does not thrash the reaper, and its\n85% alarm threshold (3.4 GiB) below it so this configuration would have *named* `/var/tmp` before\nit reached its incident size.\n\n## Scope\nIn scope: the cap script (tmpfiles drop-in, reaper units, quota application), its `compose-up.sh`\nwiring, the observability metrics, the Terraform alarms, the runbook, and tests. Out of scope:\n`/var/gerrit` data-volume probe policy (sibling `scowlful-bovine-dragonfly`), the review scratch\nvolume, Docker cache, journald, and writable container layers (S5).\n\n## Grounding\n- `infra/scripts/observability.sh` §2f/§2g are the per-generator precedents; `pct_of_cap` and the\n  `bounded` wrapper are reused rather than re-spelled.\n- `infra/scripts/journald-cap.sh` is the single-source-of-truth cap-script shape\n  (`--print-env` / `--check-active` / `--install`) this follows.\n- `infra/terraform/monitoring_autodeploy.tf` holds the sibling generator alarms.\n- `infra/runbooks/review-bot-ops.md` already names\n  `/var/tmp/rebar-evidence/<ticket>-<stamp>/` as sanctioned scratch (task 3e92), so the reaper\n  descends into that directory and treats each investigation separately.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [x] `/var/tmp` has documented age cleanup installed on every deploy.\n- [x] `/var/tmp` has a byte budget that is actively enforced — by an XFS project quota where the\n      kernel is accounting, and otherwise by a bounded oldest-first reaper timer.\n- [x] The implementation states, in the tree, why XFS project quota cannot be enabled without a\n      reboot on AL2023 root, and what the reaper does *not* guarantee (its fill-rate assumption,\n      quantified, and its grace window).\n- [x] The enforcement path publishes an **enforcement-alive heartbeat** on every probe tick\n      (`var_tmp_cleanup_active`, 1/0, following the bff5 convention), so a silently-dead\n      timer or a stale drop-in is detectable BEFORE saturation rather than reading as nominal.\n- [x] A second heartbeat (`var_tmp_hard_quota_in_effect`) says which regime is live, so\n      \"bounded\" is a reading rather than a claim.\n- [x] Observability publishes `/var/tmp` usage (`var_tmp_bytes`, `var_tmp_used_percent`), only\n      on a successful measurement, through the script's bounded wrapper.\n- [x] Terraform adds dedicated alarms for saturation and for cleanup-not-active, both wired to\n      the shared SNS topic with `treat_missing_data = \"breaching\"`.\n- [x] Runbook documents safe remediation and repeats the prohibition on `/var/gerrit` as generic\n      investigation scratch.\n- [x] Tests cover ENFORCEMENT BEHAVIOUR, not only config presence: a tree written past the\n      budget is demonstrably reclaimed, oldest-first, with the grace window and the\n      evidence-directory rule honoured — plus config rendering, metric emission, and alarm\n      wiring.\n- [x] Tests are given teeth by seeded defects: perturbing the cap, the alarm's missing-data\n      treatment, the grace window, the descend rule, the heartbeat's timer check and the probe's\n      silence-on-failure each turns a test RED.\n- [x] Portability criterion: uses Linux host-level facilities and local tests; no GitHub-only CI\n      signal is introduced."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1788586546059288001,"uuid":"2528f064-e897-46f5-94dd-3724aa3384b9"}